Subject: [PATCH] compose: stop assuming a GPU exists

PlatformDefaultBackend() returns "vulkan" on Linux, and the anchor draw filters
the catalogue by that token. So on a Linux host the compositor selects
linux-vulkan-nvidia-adf287b8f0ee, a capability cluster measured on a real
GeForce, and hands its limits, extension list and shader precision to a page.
Nothing checked whether the host has a GPU. On a server that does not, ANGLE
falls back to SwiftShader and the page gets an NVIDIA capability table in front
of a software rasteriser.

That is the one contradiction in this area a fork can actually close, and it is
not subtle. Measured on this machine, through the shipped binary, the same
fragment-shader workload runs at 2516 giga-iterations per second on ANGLE/Metal
and 9.5 on SwiftShader; small draw calls run at 2.4 million per second against
65 thousand. A detector does not need a table of reference devices for that, and
dividing by a CPU baseline does not wash it out, because the CPU baseline was
identical in both runs. Readback separates them too: the same WebGL scene and
the same 2D canvas hash differently on the two paths.

Nothing here touches render timing, and nothing should. The only ways to close a
timing gap are to make software rendering fast or to slow hardware down, and a
deliberate timing adjustment is a new observable in its own right. What can be
fixed is the claim, and the fix is to stop making one the host cannot back.

ProbeHostCapability is the right place because it is the only thing that
establishes the backend, and every consumer reads its answer: the anchor
servability filter, the explain report, and the limitations the report prints.
Fixing the caller would leave the next caller wrong.

Three cases, in order of how much they know:

--disable-gpu rules out every hardware backend, so the launch has stated the
software rasteriser. It outranks --use-angle, because naming a hardware backend
does not bring hardware back.

Failing that, the host is asked whether it has a graphics device at all. On
Linux that is a DRM render node: /dev/dri/renderD128 and up. A cloud instance
or container without GPU passthrough has no /dev/dri, and a server with nothing
but an EFI framebuffer has /dev/dri/card0 and no render node, which is why the
render node and not the directory is the fact that decides it. One access() on
the directory answers the common case; a host that has the directory costs at
most eight more.

The answer is one-way and is meant to be. Absence means ANGLE will fall back to
software. Presence means only that it probably will not: a render node with no
usable driver behind it still ends in software. Being wrong in the presence
direction leaves the behaviour that exists today, while being wrong in the
absence direction is exactly what puts a discrete GPU claim in front of a
software rasteriser -- so the check only ever moves the answer towards software,
and --use-angle remains the way to state the backend by hand. Windows and macOS
keep the platform default, because there is no equivalent single syscall there;
answering it would take a D3D11 or Metal device creation, which is the GPU
process's job.

access() rather than PathExists() because ProbeHostCapability's header promises
no blocking I/O and runs on the startup path. access() carries no blocking-call
assertion. R_OK|W_OK because the GPU process opens the node read-write, so a
node it cannot open is not a device it can use.

What happens next was patch 0081's existing policy: no anchor in the compiled
catalogue was captured through SwiftShader, so the draw finds nothing servable,
says so, and returns nothing -- which means host inheritance for the whole
profile. I judged that a catalogue gap rather than a code one and left it alone.
Patch 0093, immediately after this one in the series, shows that half of it was
a code gap after all, and keeps every non-GPU axis instead of discarding the
profile. The remaining half looked like data: a swiftshader-backend anchor is
constructible from T0 evidence already in the tree, since
resources/fingerprints/raw/stock-linux-20260907T150627Z.json is a stock
Chromium 152 Linux capture through SwiftShader with the full parameter tables,
extension lists and precision values.

That anchor was then built, and this paragraph used to end "with that anchor
present this change selects it, and a GPU-less host gets a composed software
identity rather than an inherited one" -- as though that were the goal reached.
WITHDRAWN. It was the defect. Selecting it handed a page
"ANGLE (Google, Vulkan 1.3.0 (SwiftShader Device (Subzero) (0x0000C0DE)),
SwiftShader driver)" on the deployment target's commonest configuration, with no
per-seed entropy at all because that anchor has one member. Patch 0102 excludes
it from the drawn candidate set on every host and makes the claimed OS select
the cluster instead. What this patch contributes after 0102 is the backend FACT
and its basis string, which the explain report prints and which
--fingerprint=host still acts on; what it no longer does is decide the identity.

The report now says which inference was made rather than asserting the platform
default, because after this change the platform default is no longer the only
one. On a Linux host with no render node the explain line becomes "swiftshader
(no DRM render node on this host, not probed)". Measured before the change, on
this Mac, it reads "metal (platform default, not probed)"; a Linux server with
no GPU would have read "vulkan (platform default, not probed)" while rendering
in software, which is the whole bug.

No new switch and no new page-visible value. Nothing changes on a host with a
GPU, and that is structural rather than measured: HostHasNoGpuDevice() compiles
to `return false` off Linux, and on Linux it returns false as soon as one render
node opens, so a host with a GPU reaches exactly the branch it reached before.
Measured before the change on this Mac, every default configuration -- headed,
--headless=new, composed, and --fingerprint=host -- selects ANGLE/Metal,
composes macos-metal-apple-850a91233555, and agrees on the renderer string, the
limit tables and all four readback digests.

Compile-unverified: this cannot be built in this session. What is established is
that it applies, that the behaviour it changes was measured on the shipped
binary before it, and that the branch it adds is unreachable on a host with a
GPU by inspection. Whether it compiles, and what a Linux server composes under
it, are V1 and V3 and neither has been run.

Ledger: headless.gpu-stack, gpuinfo.gl-renderer, gpu.angle-backend.
--- a/base/apostate/host_capability.h
+++ b/base/apostate/host_capability.h
@@ -38,16 +38,26 @@
   // "vulkan", "gl" or "swiftshader".
   std::string graphics_backend;
 
-  // True when `graphics_backend` is the platform's default rather than an
-  // observed fact. It always is, unless --use-angle named one explicitly.
+  // True when `graphics_backend` was reasoned about rather than stated. It is
+  // false only when the launch named the backend itself -- --use-angle picking
+  // one, or --disable-gpu ruling every hardware backend out.
   //
   // A real backend probe requires the GPU process, and the GPU process is a
   // child: A3 requires the profile to be fully materialised before the first
-  // renderer starts, so no child exists yet when this runs. The platform
-  // default is therefore the best available fact at composition time, and the
-  // explain report says so rather than implying a measurement happened.
+  // renderer starts, so no child exists yet when this runs. An inference from
+  // host facts is therefore the best available answer at composition time, and
+  // the explain report says which inference it was rather than implying a
+  // measurement happened.
   bool graphics_backend_is_inferred = true;
 
+  // How `graphics_backend` was established, as a short phrase for the explain
+  // report and the composition's limitations: "the platform default",
+  // "--use-angle", "--disable-gpu" or "no DRM render node on this host".
+  //
+  // Carried rather than recomputed at the point of display because only the
+  // probe knows which branch it took.
+  std::string graphics_backend_basis;
+
   // The host OS, as a fingerprint platform token: "windows", "macos" or
   // "linux". Empty on a platform the model has no persona for.
   std::string platform;
